@prefix orexis: <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix onboarding: <http://example.org/orexis/onboarding#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

<> a orexis:OntologyGraph .    # this document is one graph, and this is what it is

#  OREXIS ONBOARDING.
#  THE KINDS OF DOCUMENT ONBOARDING READS AND NO AGENT DOES (a-documents-kind-says-who-reads-it).
#  A kind says who reads a document, and every reader loads only the kinds it reads: the agent's
#  boot passes over a graph whose kind its T-Box does not declare. These are declared here, beside
#  the code that reads them and outside `agent/`, which the image does not copy — so no agent's
#  vocabulary can say what they are, and its ignorance of them is structural rather than a list of
#  file names kept out of its filesystem.

onboarding:HardwareGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Hardware graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The boards, pins, parts and wire colours a world is built from, read by `orexis-firmware` alone to write a board's config.h. The GRAPH has a kind and the parts inside it stay untyped, since a vocabulary one string-filling reader uses checks nothing (knowledge/domain/onboarding/onboarding.md)." .

onboarding:DeploymentGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Deployment graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What must be running for a world to be a society and where each thing answers - a broker's address, a series store, a container's image - read by onboarding alone and handed to an agent as environment. Never a credential (knowledge/domain/onboarding/deployment.md)." .

#  THE INSTALLATION'S WORDS (knowledge/domain/onboarding/deployment.md). The installation is the
#  deployment graph of what every world shares, `infra/installation.ttl`; these are what it says
#  of each shared service. An address is `schema:url`, as a broker's already was.

onboarding:SeriesStore a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Series store" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where every agent's series is written, a bucket of its own in the store's organisation - InfluxDB 2 today. `orexis-influx` mints buckets and tokens in it and `orexis-compose` tells each agent where it is (knowledge/domain/kernel/series.md)." .

onboarding:serves a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:label "serves" ;
    rdfs:domain onboarding:SeriesStore ; rdfs:range onboarding:SeriesPurpose ;
    rdfs:comment "A purpose a series store is told to every agent for: each purpose is served by exactly one store, and `orexis-compose` tells an agent that store's url and organisation under the purpose's keys, `INFLUX_<PURPOSE>_URL` and `_ORG`. Two purposes may name one store, by coincidence and not by design (knowledge/domain/kernel/series.md)." .

onboarding:SeriesPurpose a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Series purpose" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a series is written for, which decides the bucket it goes to, the credential that opens it and how long it keeps a point. Two, and the agent's code names each (`agent.series`): history and metrics." .

onboarding:History a onboarding:SeriesPurpose ; rdfs:label "history" ;
    rdfs:comment "What happened: every observation and every step taken and how it ended, contributed by the package that decides each. The record, kept for good, in the bucket the one series had before purposes." .

onboarding:Metrics a onboarding:SeriesPurpose ; rdfs:label "metrics" ;
    rdfs:comment "How the agent is doing, written every pass: what each loaded package's metric selects answer and the runtime's own three figures. A bucket of its own, opened for writing alone, and kept as long as `onboarding:retentionDays` says." .

onboarding:retentionDays a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:label "retention days" ;
    rdfs:domain onboarding:SeriesPurpose ;
    rdfs:comment "How many days a purpose's buckets keep a point, stated by the installation; absent is for ever. `orexis-influx` sets it on a bucket it makes and puts it right on one it finds kept otherwise." .

onboarding:SeriesView a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Series view" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the series is drawn for a person - the operator's view of every world at once, Grafana today. `orexis-infra-compose` runs it on the port its url states." .

onboarding:organisation a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:label "organisation" ;
    rdfs:domain onboarding:SeriesStore ;
    rdfs:comment "The organisation on a series store that every bucket is minted in and every agent is told of. Where, not what may be done there: the token is a credential and is in no graph." .

onboarding:image a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:label "image" ;
    rdfs:comment "The container image a shared service runs, pinned where a newer one would change what the service guarantees - read by `orexis-infra-compose`." .

onboarding:allocatesFrom a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:label "allocates from" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the installation allocates a broker the urls no world asserts for it: one url per scheme, each at the first port of its run, and a broker is given the lowest slot n at which every one of them, its port raised by n, is free on its host. What is allocated is a derived deployment graph, `infra/installation.derived.ttl`, and an allocation made is kept, so adding a world moves no other's port. An asserted url always wins (knowledge/decisions/a-documents-kind-says-who-reads-it.md, its amendment)." .
